# 33. DLL SEARCH ORDER

Windows applications may load DLLs based on search rules.

A vulnerable application may load an unintended DLL if:

- Search paths are unsafe
- A writable directory is searched
- An attacker can place a malicious/replacement DLL in that location

Concept:

```text
Privileged Application
        ↓
DLL Search
        ↓
Writable directory
        ↓
Unexpected DLL
        ↓
Potential privilege escalation
```

---

# 34. DLL HIJACKING VS DLL SIDE-LOADING

These terms are related but not identical.

### DLL Hijacking

An application loads an unintended DLL because of unsafe search behavior.

### DLL Side-Loading

A legitimate executable loads a malicious/replacement DLL placed where Windows searches for the expected DLL.

Exam point:

> Both involve unsafe DLL loading, but the exact mechanism matters.

